Title: Clinical Q&A about the case report: TAV-in-TAV-in-TAV after treated endocarditis: procedural strategy, imaging planning and 1-year outcome

### [Differential Diagnosis] In an older man with prior TAV-in-TAV who re-presents with dyspnoea, a mean gradient of ~50 mm Hg and moderate-to-severe intraprosthetic regurgitation 18 months after Enterococcus faecalis prosthetic valve endocarditis, what are the leading causes of transcatheter aortic prosthesis dysfunction you would include in the differential (at least four), and which features in this case help you prioritize among them?

Leading causes to consider include structural (degenerative) leaflet dysfunction, prosthesis thrombosis, recurrent or residual infective endocarditis with leaflet destruction, pannus or fibrotic overgrowth/adhesion, and mechanical causes related to frame underexpansion/prosthesis‑in‑prosthesis (patient–prosthesis mismatch or leaflet impingement). Paravalvular dehiscence/annular disruption is another cause but is less consistent with isolated intraprosthetic regurgitation.

How to prioritize in this patient:

1) Structural degeneration / leaflet fibrosis with mechanical restriction — highest on the list. The clinical course, with prior bioprosthetic degeneration, a documented episode of PVE (which can accelerate leaflet damage), and MSCT/TTE showing two overlapping stented valves with a severely reduced effective orifice (minimal lumen 13.7 mm, AVA 0.5 cm2) all point to fixed leaflet immobility and tissue fibrosis as the dominant mechanism causing combined high gradients and intraprosthetic regurgitation.

2) Frame underexpansion / prosthesis‑in‑prosthesis haemodynamic compromise (PPM) — also very likely and often coexists with leaflet dysfunction. The evolution after TAV‑in‑TAV frequently narrows the effective orifice; the small measured lumen and very high gradients are compatible with reduced annular area from overlapping frames and incomplete expansion of the Evolut inside the original device.

3) Recurrent or residual PVE with leaflet destruction or perforation — possible but less likely here. The patient had treated Enterococcus faecalis endocarditis 18 months earlier; however, preprocedural TEE and MSCT reported no vegetations or paravalvular complications and blood cultures were negative, arguing against active infection as the cause of the current dysfunction. Nonetheless prior infection could have caused irreversible leaflet damage that manifests now.

4) Prosthetic valve thrombosis — should be considered because it causes sudden rises in gradient and can produce restricted leaflet motion; it is more common in the months after implantation but can occur later. It is typically diagnosed by hypoattenuated leaflet thickening (HALT) and reduced leaflet motion on gated CT or by response to anticoagulation. In this case thrombosis is possible but less favored because CT and TEE done during workup did not report HALT or suggest thrombus, and the time course plus prior degeneration/PVE make structural causes more likely.

5) Pannus / fibrotic overgrowth or leaflet tear/prolapse — pannus is a slower, progressive process producing stenosis and sometimes regurgitation; leaflet tear/prolapse (mechanical failure) can cause isolated regurgitation. Either could contribute, but the imaging and clinical history (progressive stenosis with secondary regurgitation, multiple layered frames) point primarily to restricted leaflets and frame-related narrowing rather than an acute leaflet tear.

Finally, paravalvular dehiscence is unlikely because the regurgitation is described as intraprosthetic and no paravalvular leak or annular complications were seen on TEE/MSCT.

Practical next steps driven by this differential are: review gated contrast CT for HALT/thrombus and leaflet motion, correlate with TEE for perforation or vegetations, and consider a therapeutic trial of anticoagulation only if imaging suggests thrombosis. If imaging supports fixed structural restriction and frame underexpansion/PPM, reintervention planning (as was done) is appropriate, with attention to coronary protection and strategies to optimise effective orifice.

### [Clinical Pearls] Before considering a redo TAVI after treated prosthetic valve endocarditis, what clinical, microbiological and imaging criteria should be met to reasonably exclude active infection and proceed safely?

When you consider a repeat transcatheter valve implantation after treated prosthetic valve endocarditis, the decision must be driven first and foremost by confident exclusion of ongoing infection. Implanting additional prosthetic material into an infected field risks persistent seeding, recurrent endocarditis and catastrophic prosthesis failure; conversely, delaying a needed intervention in a high‑risk patient carries its own harms. Practically, a reasonable threshold to proceed is met when clinical, microbiological and imaging data together make active infection unlikely — and this determination should be documented and endorsed by infectious diseases, imaging and heart‑team specialists.

Clinical criteria
The patient should be clinically well from an infectious standpoint: afebrile for a sustained period, without new constitutional symptoms (fever, night sweats, weight loss) or localizing infectious findings, and with improvement or stability of heart‑failure status that is attributable to structural valve dysfunction rather than ongoing sepsis. Any extracardiac infectious source (eg, urologic, dental, gastrointestinal) must be identified and treated or shown not to be active. The overall clinical course should be consistent with resolution rather than smouldering disease.

Microbiological criteria
Documented completion of an appropriate, guideline‑directed antimicrobial course for the identified pathogen is essential (eg, full 6 weeks for Enterococcus prosthetic valve endocarditis unless alternative duration is specified by ID). Blood cultures must be repeatedly negative: multiple sets (at least three sets) demonstrating no bacteremia after therapy, and ideally negative cultures both while on therapy and after completion. Many teams require a period free of bacteremia off intravenous antibiotics (commonly 7–14 days) before elective reintervention to reduce false reassurance from culture suppression by ongoing antibiotics. In addition, inflammatory markers (CRP, ESR) should show a downward trend or normalization consistent with clinical resolution. If long‑term suppressive therapy was considered, the team must weigh risks: absence of need for indefinite suppression supports proceeding.

Imaging criteria
Transesophageal echocardiography (TEE) should show no residual vegetations, no new mobile masses, and no paravalvular complications (abscess, pseudoaneurysm, fistula) or progressive dehiscence. High‑resolution gated CT (MSCT) should demonstrate absence of perivalvular collections, pseudoaneurysm or tissue disruption and be used to plan anatomy for the new valve. When available and interpretation will change management, nuclear imaging (FDG‑PET/CT) can be helpful: lack of focal abnormal FDG uptake around the prosthesis supports quiescence of infection (bearing in mind false positives early after implant and false negatives with low‑grade infections). All imaging should be recent (eg, within days to a few weeks of the planned procedure) and correlated with clinical status.

Process and team judgment
Clear documentation of infectious‑disease clearance that states the infection is considered cured and names the evidence supporting that conclusion is required. The heart team should confirm that the urgency of intervention balances the residual risk of occult infection. If there is any imaging or microbiologic uncertainty, delay for further evaluation or consider surgical options if infection control is incomplete. Finally, plan peri‑procedural antimicrobial prophylaxis and close post‑procedure surveillance (clinical follow‑up, serial blood cultures if clinically indicated, early imaging) because recurrence risk, although low when criteria are met, carries high morbidity.

Applying these principles to the presented case: the team had a completed guideline‑directed 6‑week course for Enterococcus, subsequent negative blood cultures, TEE showing no vegetations or paravalvular complication, MSCT without perivalvular collections, no suppressive antibiotics, and ID sign‑off — a reasonable bundle of evidence to conclude absence of active PVE and proceed with a carefully planned TAVI.

### [Pathophysiology] In this case, E. faecalis PVE was managed with a 6-week ampicillin-based regimen. Briefly explain the pathophysiologic mechanisms by which a successfully treated episode of PVE can still lead to late prosthetic valve dysfunction (stenosis and/or regurgitation) months later.

Teaching pearl:

Successful eradication of bacteremia does not erase the mechanical and biological injury that infective endocarditis (IE) inflicts on a prosthetic valve. In prosthetic‑valve endocarditis the organisms and the host inflammatory response damage leaflet tissue, the sewing ring–annulus interface and the surrounding aortic wall; antibiotic cure removes the pathogen but the structural sequelae evolve over weeks to months and can produce late stenosis, regurgitation, or both.

Specifically, valve dysfunction after treated Enterococcus faecalis PVE can occur by several pathophysiologic pathways that often coexist. Infection may produce focal leaflet tissue destruction or micro‑perforations that are not apparent after therapy; healing of those defects with fibrosis, retraction and scar shortens and stiffens cusps, causing both central regurgitation (from poor coaptation) and progressive stenosis (from restricted opening). Deep or subannular infection and abscess formation can weaken the annulus or cause partial dehiscence; even after sterilization the fibrotic remodeling and loss of normal valve‑annulus apposition produce paravalvular leaks and altered valve geometry that worsen over time. Persistent microbial biofilm seeded on prosthetic material can be clinically silent yet promote ongoing low‑grade damage and accelerated structural degeneration of leaflets and glutaraldehyde‑fixed tissue. Finally, the intense inflammatory milieu accelerates pannus formation, calcification and thrombus deposition on leaflets or frame, reducing effective orifice area and increasing gradients.

Translate this to the case: the patient’s E. faecalis PVE was treated, but the infection likely caused leaflet injury and/or annular damage followed by fibrotic remodeling and possibly accelerated structural degeneration of the transcatheter leaflets and frames. That process explains the interval development of both increased transvalvular gradient (stenosis from leaflet stiffness/pannus/calcification) and intraprosthetic regurgitation (from retraction or small perforations or altered seating), months after microbiologic cure.

Clinical implications are practical: eradication of infection is necessary but not sufficient—patients with PVE require prolonged imaging surveillance (TEE, CT), attention to changing gradients or new regurgitation, and early multidisciplinary reassessment because structural sequelae may mandate reintervention despite microbiologic cure.

### [Management] Contemporary ESC guidance summarized in this case states that surgery remains the standard for PVE with heart failure or uncontrolled infection, yet this patient underwent TAV-in-TAV-in-TAV. Which patient- and anatomy-specific factors would justify favoring a transcatheter strategy over surgery in such a scenario?

The contemporary ESC guidance rightly prioritises surgery for prosthetic valve endocarditis (PVE) when there is heart failure, uncontrolled infection, root/abscess involvement, or other surgical indications. But guidelines also emphasise individualisation. In practice a transcatheter approach can be justified when a clear combination of patient‑specific and anatomy‑specific factors makes open surgery excessively risky or unlikely to improve outcome and when imaging confirms that a redo‑TAVI can be performed with an acceptable procedural risk and expected haemodynamic benefit. The key elements that would tip the balance toward TAVI in a patient like this are:

First, patient factors that make surgery prohibitive. Advanced age, severe comorbidity burden (eg, frailty, COPD, diabetes, prior multi‑vessel PCI, prior pacemaker, recurrent GI bleeding), a very high calculated operative risk (eg, EuroSCORE II in the high 20s), and prior intravascular or chest interventions that render re‑sternotomy hazardous argue strongly for a less invasive option. If explantation would likely require complex root and ascending aortic replacement rather than straightforward valve replacement, the incremental surgical morbidity and mortality must be weighed heavily.

Second, infection control. TAVI should only be considered after documented eradication of infection: completed appropriate antibiotic therapy, persistently negative blood cultures, no clinical or laboratory signs of ongoing sepsis, and high‑quality imaging (TEE and MSCT) demonstrating absence of vegetations, paravalvular abscesses, fistulae or prosthetic dehiscence. Active or uncontrolled infection remains a surgical indication.

Third, favourable anatomy on high‑resolution CT and TEE that predicts low procedural risk and acceptable long‑term haemodynamics. This includes adequate valve‑to‑coronary distance and sinus of Valsalva dimensions that make coronary obstruction unlikely (or feasible to protect), the ability to achieve commissural alignment to preserve coronary access, an identified landing zone without paravalvular disruption, and sufficient residual lumen or frame geometry to accept a new valve with anticipated effective orifice area that will not result in prohibitive prosthesis–patient mismatch. In sequential TAV‑in‑TAV cases one must also confirm that frame overlap and leaflet configuration permit deployment (and, if needed, safe leaflet fixation or laceration strategies), and that post‑dilation or partial expansion of the prior frame can reasonably improve flow without undue risk.

Fourth, expected clinical benefit and patient preference. The intervention should be expected to relieve symptoms and improve cardiac function (eg, prior improvement in EF after the second TAV), and the patient (after informed shared decision‑making) should accept the non‑surgical strategy given the risks of both options.

Putting it together: a transcatheter strategy is reasonable when infection is clearly eradicated, the patient is at prohibitive surgical risk or surgery would require extensive root replacement, preprocedural CT/TEE show low coronary obstruction and an acceptable landing zone with predicted haemodynamics, commissural alignment/future coronary access are achievable, and the projected symptomatic and functional benefit justifies the procedure. In that setting a carefully planned, imaging‑guided TAV‑in‑TAV (even TAV‑in‑TAV‑in‑TAV) performed by a multidisciplinary heart team can be an appropriate alternative to surgery.

### [Clinical Pearls] For TAV-in-TAV-in-TAV planning, which preprocedural CT measurements and anatomic relationships most strongly predict risk of coronary obstruction, and what threshold values would prompt you to consider coronary protection or leaflet modification (e.g., BASILICA)?

The critical question in any repeat TAV procedure — and especially in a TAV‑in‑TAV‑in‑TAV situation — is not simply “how tall are the coronaries?” but “what will the new valve and the displaced old leaflets/frames do to the ostia in three dimensions?” Pre‑procedural CT must therefore move beyond single numbers to a small set of measurements and relationships that best predict displacement of leaflet tissue or formation of a neo‑skirt that will obstruct coronary flow.

First, measure and simulate a virtual valve implantation on contrast gated CT (ideally with dedicated valve‑in‑valve software) to generate the predicted neo‑skirt and its relation to the coronaries. From that model the two most predictive metrics are the valve‑to‑coronary (VTC) distance and the expected neo‑skirt height relative to the coronary ostium. A VTC (distance from the anticipated outer leaflet/neo‑skirt to the coronary ostium) <4 mm is high risk for obstruction; 4–6 mm is intermediate risk and should prompt strong consideration of protection or leaflet modification; >6 mm is generally low risk, assuming other features are favorable.

Second, contextualize VTC with coronary ostial height and sinus geometry. Coronary ostial height <10 mm is associated with higher obstruction risk; heights 10–12 mm are borderline and require attention to VTC and sinus size. Small sinuses of Valsalva (SOV) or narrow sinotubular junction (STJ) dramatically increase risk because there is no room for displaced leaflets: SOV diameter <30–32 mm (depending on prosthesis size) and a short or narrow STJ raise concern. If SOV diameter is small and VTC is borderline, treat as high risk.

Third, account for the prior valve(s) anatomy and frame/leaflet configuration. Supra‑annular bioprostheses (e.g., self‑expanding Evolut) with tall leaflets and high stent frames, prior commissural misalignment, and cumulative stacked frames increase the chance that leaflet tissue will be held up against the coronary ostia and form a persistent neo‑skirt. Measure the leaflet length and predicted maximum extent of displaced leaflet tissue; if displaced leaflet reaches at or above the coronary ostium on the simulation, obstruction risk is high even if ostial height alone seems acceptable.

Practical thresholds I use in planning (and that should trigger coronary protection or BASILICA discussion)
- VTC <4 mm: high risk — plan BASILICA (if anatomy and operator experience permit) or mandatory upfront coronary protection with a guidewire/balloon and ready covered stent.  
- VTC 4–6 mm or coronary height 10–12 mm with small SOV (≤32 mm) or narrow STJ: intermediate risk — strongly favor coronary protection and consider BASILICA if simulation predicts leaflet contact; discuss chimney/stent bailout strategy if BASILICA not feasible.  
- Coronary height <10 mm or predicted neo‑skirt at or above ostium regardless of VTC: treat as high risk and favour leaflet laceration (BASILICA) if feasible; if not feasible, plan for protection/backup chimney stenting or reconsider strategy (surgical explant if possible).  
- SOV diameter <30–32 mm or STJ that will trap leaflets: escalate to BASILICA or protection even when VTC borderline.

Other practical considerations that modify decision‑making include commissural alignment (poor alignment increases obstruction and makes future access harder), ability to deliver BASILICA safely across multiple frames, and coronary artery disease status (if coronaries are critical territory, lower threshold for protection). Always perform full CT simulation, include 3‑D printed or virtual fluoroscopic projections when available, and review in the heart team. If BASILICA is planned, ensure the leaflet to be lacerated can be snared/crossed through the stacked frames — when crossing is unlikely, an upfront coronary protection strategy with a prepared covered stent is prudent.

In short: use virtual implantation to get a VTC and neo‑skirt estimate; treat VTC <4 mm (or predicted neo‑skirt at/above ostium, coronary height <10 mm, or small SOV/STJ) as high risk mandating BASILICA or routine coronary protection; treat VTC 4–6 mm or borderline anatomy as intermediate risk and lean toward protection or BASILICA after heart‑team deliberation.

### [Management] This team selected a self-expanding, supra-annular Acurate neo2 valve and aimed for high implantation with commissural alignment at the level of the prior Evolut leaflets. What device and deployment features make this strategy advantageous in a triple-stented anatomy, and what are the intended haemodynamic and coronary-access benefits?

Choose a valve and a deployment technique to solve two linked problems created by layered prostheses: (1) a narrowed inflow caused by inward‑folded or constrained prior leaflets that reduces effective orifice area and raises gradients, and (2) the risk that a new neo‑skirt or additional frame will cover the coronary ostia and make future coronary access impossible.

The team’s choice of a self‑expanding, supra‑annular Acurate neo2 and a high, commissure‑aligned implant addresses both problems by design and by deployment mechanics. Device features that matter here are the supra‑annular leaflet position (leaflets sit above the frame), a relatively low and open frame with radiopaque commissural markers, and a top‑down controlled deployment that facilitates precise axial and rotational positioning. As a self‑expanding valve it exerts continuous radial force that can partially expand and re‑shape the existing Evolut frame without the shock of a rigid balloon fracture; controlled post‑dilation can then augment expansion. The low frame/short skirt and the ability to orient commissures relative to the prior valve leaflets permit a deliberate “high” landing at the level of the Evolut leaflets so that the old leaflets are pressed outward and become part of the flow lumen rather than forming a new obstructing skirt.

The intended haemodynamic benefits follow directly: supra‑annular leaflets and fixation at the prior‑leaflet level maximize the residual effective orifice area, reduce leaflet infolding, and improve leaflet excursion, all of which lower transvalvular gradients and reduce the risk of severe prosthesis‑patient mismatch. The intended coronary‑access benefits derive from minimizing a neo‑skirt below the coronary ostia, keeping the sinuses and valve‑to‑coronary distances more favourable, and aligning commissures so catheter engagement of the coronaries is less impeded by overlapping struts. In short, this strategy trades precise axial/rotational control and favorable leaflet geometry for improved immediate haemodynamics and higher likelihood of safe, repeat coronary interventions in a triple‑stented anatomy.

A final practical caveat: even with these features and meticulous CT‑guided alignment, cumulative mesh burden still poses residual risk to coronary access and may complicate future interventions, so the strategy is mitigation, not elimination, of those long‑term risks.

### [Morning Report] Immediately after the third TAVI, TTE showed mean gradient 67 mm Hg, AVA 0.9 cm² (indexed 0.4 cm²/m²) and DVI 0.24 with no regurgitation—yet by 1 month the mean gradient fell to 27 mm Hg and AVA rose to 1.1 cm². How would you interpret the initial haemodynamics (including the likelihood of severe patient–prosthesis mismatch), outline the differential for high early gradients after TAVI-in-TAV-in-TAV, and explain the subsequent improvement?

The immediate post‑implant TTE looked alarming and, taken at face value, met criteria for severe prosthesis‑patient mismatch (mean gradient 67 mm Hg, AVA 0.9 cm2, DVI 0.24). But in the context of a third transcatheter valve implanted into two prior frames that were already constrained, those numbers are best interpreted as “functional” or “dynamic” obstruction rather than proof of an irreversibly undersized valve. In other words, the early haemodynamics reflected an acute reduction in effective orifice produced by the interaction of three frames and leaflets at the time of implantation—not necessarily a fixed true PPM that will persist long‑term.

When gradients are unexpectedly high immediately after complex TAV‑in‑TAV procedures the differential is dominated by mechanical and measurement issues. The most likely causes are under‑expansion of the newly implanted nitinol frame (temporary incomplete radial expansion), inward displacement or pinning of native/prosthetic leaflets between frames (reduced leaflet excursion), suboptimal implantation depth or frame malapposition, and incomplete effective orifice because the previously implanted Evolut frame remained constricted. Less likely but important considerations are acute valve thrombosis (rare immediately post‑implant), prosthetic leaflet immobility from entrapment in residual material, and Doppler/pressure‑recovery or technical measurement artifact (high heart rate, non‑steady flows, transducer alignment). In this case leaflet motion and positioning were reported as mobile and there was no regurgitation, arguing against leaflet destruction or major malposition but not excluding transient mechanical restriction.

The fall in gradient by one month is readily explained by known mechanical behavior of self‑expanding frames and the effect of aggressive post‑dilatation performed at the index procedure. Self‑expanding nitinol stents often continue to expand over hours to days; balloon post‑dilatation can partially fracture or radially expand an inner constrained frame (here the Evolut) and relieve circumferential compression. That progressive expansion restores leaflet coaptation geometry and enlarges the effective orifice. Concurrently, any trapped leaflets can recoil to a more mobile configuration as the scaffolding settles, and haemodynamics normalize as heart rate and loading conditions stabilize after the procedure. Minor Doppler measurement variability also contributes. Taken together these mechanisms explain why a gradient that appeared consistent with “severe PPM” immediately after TAVI resolved into a much more acceptable gradient (mean 27 mm Hg, AVA 1.1 cm2) by one month.

The clinical teaching point is twofold: first, in multi‑frame TAV‑in‑TAV procedures an elevated immediate post‑implant gradient commonly reflects dynamic mechanical factors and does not always equal fixed severe PPM; second, management should combine careful intra‑procedural assessment (fluoroscopy/echocardiography), considered use of post‑dilatation to optimise expansion, and short‑interval surveillance rather than reflexive reintervention. If gradients remain high after a period of observation, then true structural PPM, leaflet thrombosis or malposition should be investigated and treated accordingly.

### [Clinical Pearls] Which echocardiographic indices are most useful for identifying severe patient–prosthesis mismatch after TAVI, and what practical cutoff values are typically used for indexed effective orifice area and Doppler velocity index?

The key echocardiographic tools for detecting severe patient–prosthesis mismatch (PPM) after TAVI are the continuity‑equation–derived effective orifice area (EOA) indexed to body surface area (BSA), the Doppler velocity index (DVI, sometimes called dimensionless index), and corroborating Doppler gradients—interpreted in the context of flow status. These indices are complementary: indexed EOA quantifies the anatomic/functional orifice relative to patient size, DVI avoids errors from LVOT diameter measurement, and mean gradient reflects the haemodynamic consequence but is flow‑dependent.

Practically, the commonly used cutoffs are:
- Indexed EOA (EOA/BSA): severe PPM ≤ 0.65 cm2/m2; moderate PPM 0.65–0.85 cm2/m2. (Values ≤0.65 indicate critically small effective valve area for the patient.)
- Doppler velocity index (DVI = VTI_LVOT / VTI_aortic or Vmax_LVOT / Vmax_aortic): severe PPM ~≤0.25; moderate PPM ~0.25–0.35. A DVI ≤0.25 is strongly suggestive of severe obstruction/PPM independent of LVOT sizing errors.

Use these with additional data: an absolute AVA <1.0 cm2 and mean transvalvular gradient >40 mm Hg support severe obstruction, but gradients can be lower in low‑flow states so rely on indexed EOA/DVI when flow is reduced. Be vigilant for measurement pitfalls—errors in LVOT diameter (especially with supra‑annular or stented valves), misalignment of Doppler beam, and changes in stroke volume after interventions (as in this case where gradients fell as flow and frame expansion improved). When discordant, integrate clinical status, stroke volume index, and imaging (CT to assess frame/neo‑orifice) to decide if the prosthesis is truly mismatched and whether reintervention is indicated.

### [Pathophysiology] Describe how balloon pre- and post-dilatation under rapid pacing can improve valve haemodynamics in TAV-in-TAV-in-TAV without intentional frame fracture. What are the mechanical effects on underexpanded frames and constrained leaflets that lower gradients?

Think of the layered TAV-in-TAV-in-TAV as a concentric set of tubes and constrained leaflets: the inner Evolut frame in this case was markedly under‑expanded (minimum lumen ~13–14 mm) and the older leaflets were folded or tethered inward so the effective orifice was tiny. Strategic balloon dilatation before and after the new valve works by simple mechanical principles rather than by “fracturing” metal.

Under rapid ventricular pacing you nearly eliminate forward flow and cardiac motion, which lets you deliver high balloon pressure safely and transiently without violent displacement of wires or devices and without generating large transvalvular pressure gradients. Pre‑dilatation does two things: it mechanically pries open the most stenotic, fibrotic leaflet folds and it exerts radial force on the under‑expanded nitinol frame producing plastic (and elastic) deformation — increasing the internal diameter enough to permit passage and better seating of the new prosthesis. That initial expansion also pushes adherent leaflet tissue and pannus aside, reducing leaflet crowding that would otherwise constrain the new valve.

Once the new supra‑annular valve is deployed, post‑dilatation under pacing accomplishes further, more controlled radial expansion of the composite construct. The balloon transmits force through the new stent into the old frame(s), producing incremental widening of the lumen, flattening of inward‑folded leaflets and improved leaflet separation from the central flow stream. The net mechanical effects are increased effective orifice area, improved leaflet mobility (less restriction/tethering), and a more circular, fuller stent geometry — all of which lower transvalvular velocity and gradient.

Clinically important corollaries are: ballooning can compress fibrous tissue and break adhesive attachments without needing to intentionally fracture the frame; supra‑annular valve selection and high implantation help keep the new leaflets out of the most constrained zone; and pacing is essential to permit high‑pressure, controlled dilation with less embolic or hemodynamic risk. The tradeoffs are the usual ones — risk of coronary compromise, annular injury, valve migration, or conduction disturbance — so careful CT planning, sizing and coronary protection considerations remain mandatory.

### [Management] In a high bleeding risk TAVI patient without another indication for oral anticoagulation, what is the preferred postprocedural antithrombotic regimen, and under what circumstances would you escalate evaluation and therapy for suspected leaflet thrombosis (e.g., 4D-CT and short-term anticoagulation)?

In a patient like this — high bleeding risk after TAVI and no other indication for long‑term oral anticoagulation — the default post‑procedural strategy is single antiplatelet therapy rather than dual therapy or routine anticoagulation. Randomised and guideline data show that dual antiplatelet therapy after TAVI increases bleeding without clear ischemic benefit; for most patients without an indication for anticoagulation, one antiplatelet agent (typically low‑dose aspirin) started immediately and continued long‑term is preferred. In a patient with very high bleeding risk, limited duration (for example indefinite low‑dose aspirin with careful attention to bleeding mitigation) or even individualized de‑escalation after the immediate periprocedural window is reasonable, but routine post‑TAVI anticoagulation is not recommended solely to prevent subclinical leaflet thrombosis.

When to escalate evaluation and treat for suspected leaflet thrombosis
Assess first with transthoracic echocardiography and the clinical picture. Escalation to advanced imaging (4D‑CT) and to a time‑limited anticoagulant trial is indicated when there is objective evidence of impaired valve function or clinical concern rather than isolated, incidental findings:

- New or unexplained rise in transvalvular gradient on serial TTE (for example a sustained increase in mean gradient of ~10 mm Hg or more from a recent baseline, or an absolute mean gradient that becomes clinically significant or accompanied by a drop in DVI/AVA), new heart‑failure symptoms out of proportion to other causes, or thromboembolic events should prompt further evaluation.
- If TTE raises concern for restricted leaflet motion or thrombus, obtain 4D‑CT (gated multidetector CT with cine review) to look for hypo‑attenuated leaflet thickening (HALT) and reduced leaflet motion (RLM), which are the imaging hallmarks of leaflet thrombosis.
- If 4D‑CT confirms HALT/RLM and the lesion is haemodynamically or clinically meaningful (elevated gradients, symptoms, or embolic phenomena), a trial of short‑term oral anticoagulation is appropriate. In practice this is usually a direct oral anticoagulant or vitamin K antagonist for about 3 months with clinical and imaging reassessment; most thrombotic HALT resolves with anticoagulation and gradients improve.
- If HALT is incidentally detected on CT but gradients are normal and the patient is asymptomatic, the approach can be conservative: observe and repeat imaging (TTE ± CT) rather than automatically anticoagulating, especially in patients at high bleeding risk.

Apply individualized risk‑benefit reasoning in every case. Prior GI bleeding or other major bleeding risks do not prohibit investigation, but they raise the threshold for therapeutic anticoagulation; in those patients reserve anticoagulation for confirmed, clinically meaningful thrombosis and use the shortest effective course with close follow‑up and multidisciplinary input (cardiology, imaging, and if needed hematology/gastroenterology). Reassess after treatment with TTE and, if necessary, repeat CT to confirm resolution before stopping anticoagulation.

### [Clinical Pearls] What procedural tactics help preserve present and future coronary access in patients who already have two transcatheter valves in place, and what trade-offs do these tactics entail?

Key teaching point: when a patient already has two transcatheter aortic valves in place, every technical choice for a repeat TAVI must balance two goals that can conflict — preserve immediate coronary perfusion and maintain practical coronary re‑access for the future — against the competing need to optimise valve haemodynamics. Thoughtful pre‑procedural CT and a targeted procedural strategy mitigate the most important risks, but each maneuver has predictable trade‑offs.

Tactics that help preserve present and future coronary access and the trade‑offs they entail

1) Preprocedural CT‑guided planning and 3‑D modelling
Why it matters: precise measurement of valve‑to‑coronary distances, sinus dimensions, frame interactions and predicted neo‑skirt location lets you choose valve type, size and implantation depth to minimise coronary obstruction and preserve a working lateral window for future PCI.
Trade‑off: time, need for expert imaging and sometimes imperfect prediction of final frame conformation when multiple stents are present.

2) Commissural alignment
Why it matters: deliberate rotational orientation of the new valve to align its commissures with the existing prosthesis preserves ostial windows through the stent cell geometry and eases future coronary cannulation.
Trade‑off: requires valves and deployment techniques that allow controlled rotation, adds procedural complexity and fluoroscopy time, and alignment may still be imperfect — leaving residual difficulty for future coronary access.

3) High implantation (implanting the new valve at or above the level of the previous leaflets)
Why it matters: keeps the neo‑skirt and displaced leaflets away from the coronary ostia, reducing immediate obstruction risk and leaving larger cells for re‑access.
Trade‑off: higher implantation can increase risk of valve migration/embolisation, paravalvular leak if sealing zone is suboptimal, and may sacrifice some effective orifice area if positioning is suboptimal; it may also reduce radial support for anchoring in some anatomies.

4) Valve selection with an eye to frame height, cell geometry and leaflet position
Why it matters: choosing a valve with lower frame height, large open cells and supra‑annular leaflets (as in this case with Acurate neo2) tends to preserve coronary windows and gives better haemodynamics in small inner diameters.
Trade‑off: some devices with favorable access profiles may provide less radial force (less ability to expand prior frames), may be off‑label in certain scenarios, or have other device‑specific risks (e.g., higher pacemaker rates or anchoring issues).

5) Coronary protection strategies (wiring ostia, pre‑positioned stents or standby PCI)
Why it matters: advancing a guidewire into the coronary (or having a stent ready) provides an immediate bailout if the ostium becomes compromised during deployment.
Trade‑off: routine wiring increases procedure time, stroke and coronary dissection risk, and if a chimney stent is deployed it creates a permanently protruding stent with higher long‑term restenosis and reintervention complexity.

6) Preventive leaflet laceration (BASILICA) in selected anatomies
Why it matters: cutting the leaflet that would otherwise form a neo‑skirt prevents obstruction while maintaining access.
Trade‑off: BASILICA adds technical complexity, requires experience, lengthens the procedure, and carries risks including stroke, coronary embolisation and perforation; its feasibility in multiple prior frames is less certain.

7) Post‑dilatation or intentional bioprosthetic frame fracture
Why it matters: post‑dilatation can help expand prior frames and improve haemodynamics, and fracture (where possible) increases inner diameter and preserves flow.
Trade‑off: high‑pressure dilatation risks annular injury, annular rupture, stroke, and may worsen coronary compromise transiently; fracture is device‑dependent (not always possible) and unpredictable in layered frames.

8) Accepting a hemodynamic compromise to prioritise coronary preservation
Why it matters: deliberate choices (smaller valve, higher implant, limited oversizing) can avoid coronary obstruction.
Trade‑off: higher residual gradients and prosthesis–patient mismatch, which may limit symptomatic improvement and longevity of the valve.

Clinical principle to apply: plan the lifetime strategy at the first TAVI in patients likely to need reintervention — prioritize commissural alignment, choose valves and implantation depths that preserve future access, and document fluoroscopic landmarks and rotational orientation to guide future procedures. In redo and redo‑redo settings, combine meticulous CT modelling with selective protection (wiring, BASILICA) and valve selection that balances anchoring and access, recognizing that every maneuver to reduce obstruction risk carries procedural risk or may worsen haemodynamics.

In practice, the team must individualize the approach: use CT to guide whether high implantation + a low‑frame, supra‑annular valve (accepting possible higher gradients) is preferable, or whether coronary protection/BASILICA is required despite added complexity. Shared decision‑making with the patient and multidisciplinary review are essential because the chosen trade‑offs determine both immediate safety and the feasibility of future coronary or valve interventions.

### [Morning Report] If this triple-stented aortic valve fails in the future, outline the remaining therapeutic options and their key risks, including surgical explantation and root replacement, bioprosthetic valve fracture, leaflet laceration (BASILICA), and the feasibility concerns unique to a TAV-in-TAV-in-TAV anatomy.

If this patient’s newly implanted valve fails in the future, the clinical reality is that true “options” become few and high-risk. Management must be individualized by a multidisciplinary heart team with updated CT, TEE and careful assessment of surgical candidacy. The main strategies and their key risks are:

Surgical explantation and aortic root replacement
Because the prior transcatheter frames are endothelialised and often incorporated into the aortic root, surgical removal will be technically demanding. The operation commonly requires full explant of layered stent frames, aortic root replacement (Bentall or homograft), possible coronary reimplantation and prolonged cardiopulmonary bypass. Risks include very high perioperative mortality and morbidity in this frail, comorbid patient (bleeding, stroke, acute kidney injury, prolonged ventilation), annular or ventricular injury during explant, coronary injury requiring bypass, and the possibility that complete removal is not possible without extensive aortic reconstruction. Prior endocarditis raises infectious and tissue-fraction concerns (adhesions, friable tissue), increasing complexity. Surgical therapy remains the only definitive option when obstruction, abscess, fistula, uncontrolled infection or root destruction are present, but for many elderly, high-risk patients it may be effectively prohibitive.

Bioprosthetic valve fracture (BVF) or intentional frame expansion
BVF uses high-pressure balloon inflation to crack the rigid sewing ring of surgical bioprostheses to increase effective orifice area; in transcatheter frames the concept is attempting to expand an inner frame mechanically to improve orifice. In a TAV-in-TAV-in-TAV anatomy, BVF faces technical and safety limits. Many transcatheter frames (particularly nitinol self‑expanding frames like Evolut) do not have a defined ring designed to fracture, so fracture may be impossible. Even if the outer frame gives way mechanically, risks include uncontrolled frame disruption, leaflet tearing, valve embolization, acute severe aortic regurgitation, annular rupture, coronary obstruction from displaced leaflets or frame portions, and stroke from debris. Because the final orifice is small and the layered frames transmit high radial forces to the annulus, the risk of annular injury is real. BVF is therefore rarely reliable in multiple overlapping TAVs and should be considered only after CT demonstrates an anatomy amenable to safe, controlled expansion and with surgical backup.

Leaflet laceration (BASILICA) or other commissural interventions
BASILICA was developed to lacerate a leaflet to prevent iatrogenic coronary obstruction during TAV-in‑SAV or TAV-in‑TAV. In the triple‑stented setting its feasibility is limited. Multiple overlapping leaflets and frames make traversal, positioning, and effective laceration technically difficult: the target leaflet may be crushed between frames, electrically insulated, or inaccessible to the electrified wire. Laceration may be incomplete, fail to create an adequate coronary flow channel, or damage adjacent frames. Risks include failed prevention of coronary obstruction (with catastrophic myocardial ischemia), aortic root injury, embolization of leaflet tissue, stroke, and procedure-related bleeding. In some cases, BASILICA through the outermost leaflet might be possible but may not protect against obstruction caused by deeper, rigid frame/leaflet assemblies. Finally, access for the laceration equipment may be limited by small transcatheter lumens and previous frame geometry.

Coronary protection strategies / chimney or snorkel stenting
When coronary obstruction risk is high and BASILICA is not feasible or reliable, upfront coronary protection with guidewires, undeployed stents ready to be deployed (chimney), or even prophylactic surgical bypass can be considered. In TAV-in-TAV-in-TAV the mesh density and commissural misalignment may make guidewire crossing difficult; chimney stents carry risks of stent crush, thrombosis, impaired long-term patency, and marginal coronary flow if the ostium is partially covered. These are bailout, not ideal, definitive solutions.

Repeat transcatheter valve-in-valve (another TAV-in-TAV)
Implanting another valve into an already small, triple-stented orifice will usually worsen patient–prosthesis mismatch and may not be feasible if the residual lumen is too small. Hemodynamic benefit is limited and the risk of coronary obstruction and further loss of coronary access increases. This is thus generally a temporizing or last-resort option when no other route exists.

Hybrid approaches and palliative care
For patients who remain poor surgical candidates and in whom catheter techniques are either infeasible or too risky, a pragmatic strategy may be optimized medical therapy and symptom-directed care, with consideration of palliative strategies. In selected cases, a hybrid approach (limited surgical debridement or conduit creation plus transcatheter implantation) could be considered in experienced centers.

Practical principles going forward
Decisions hinge on up-to-date high-resolution CT with 3‑D modelling to define valve-to-coronary distances, frame interactions, and annular integrity; coronary angiography and TEE; and reassessment of surgical risk. Prior attempts at commissural alignment and preservation of coronary access are meaningful now — they determine whether coronary protection or BASILICA are possible. If surgical risk improves or the patient becomes a better operative candidate, early referral to an experienced aortic root surgical team is reasonable because delayed surgery after multiple failed catheter procedures is even more hazardous.

Bottom line: definitive surgical root replacement is the only curative option when anatomy or infection mandates removal, but it carries very high risk after multiple TAVs. Catheter approaches such as BVF or BASILICA may be useful in select anatomies, but in a TAV-in-TAV-in-TAV their technical feasibility and safety are limited; they carry substantial risks of annular injury, valve embolization, coronary obstruction and stroke. Management requires individualized heart‑team deliberation, detailed CT planning, and honest discussion with the patient about procedural risk, likely durability and realistic outcomes.

